msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:267
msgid "The SVG has a 960 × 420 view box, an accessible `<title>` and a `<desc>` that includes the plot's note, inline styles, linear grid ticks or logarithmic ticks with bounded label density, the unit as the y-axis label, `x_label` under the x axis, and a legend. On a categorical axis the x values are spaced evenly in order of first appearance and at most about ten are labelled. On a linear axis they are sorted and placed by value, and a label is drawn only when it is at least 56 px from the previous one. Every point is drawn as a circle with a tooltip `series — x: y unit`. `Scaling`, `Interval` and `ChangePoint` plots also connect, per series, the mean of the points at each x value. `Heatmap` plots draw one cell per series and x category, with an opacity that grows with the mean value. An empty plot shows \"No observations\". All text is XML-escaped."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:295
msgid "`html` renders a whole document as one self-contained HTML page."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:301
msgid "The page contains a header with run id, target and schema version, then the differential report (corpus outcomes, mismatches, capability coverage matrix, minimal counterexamples), then a \"Comparisons\" section when the document has comparison rows, then one figure per plot with the plot's note in its caption. The comparison table has one row per comparison: case, scale, baseline, candidate, both medians in µs/op, the relative delta, the interval in percent, the decision as a labelled tag with its reason, the blocks used, incomplete and removed as outliers, and the row seed; the settings note is printed above it. CSS is inline, plots are inline SVG, and there are no scripts and no external resources, so the file can be archived or attached as it is. The output depends only on the document."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:327
msgid "`comparisons_text` renders the comparisons of a document as an aligned plain-text table."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:334
msgid "The text is the line `Comparisons`, the settings note, a header line and one line per row, with the columns of the HTML table plus the reason, separated by at least two spaces. Missing values are `-`; deltas and interval bounds carry an explicit sign, and numbers are rounded to three decimals. It is `\"\"` when the document has no comparison rows. `mare-mark report` prints it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:354
#: manual/tutorial/report.md:226
msgid "Logarithmic axes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/report.md:356
#: manual/tutorial/report.md:228
msgid "Scaling plots choose x and y independently. Positive finite values spanning at least 100× use base-10 logarithmic coordinates; other numeric axes remain linear, and nonnumeric x values remain categorical. Log ticks prioritize powers of ten, thinning decades for dense ranges and adding 2× and 5× ticks only when labels fit. The IR and JSON record both axis scales, and labels and notes identify log scaling."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:1
msgid "runner API"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:5
msgid "`Luna-Flow/mare_mark/runner` owns the measurement loop. You describe a benchmark case, compile it into a validated plan, and `run` it with a seed, an environment snapshot, an event sink and a validated protocol. The runner validates every implementation against an oracle before timing, calibrates the batch size, measures in balanced blocks and emits raw events. The reasons for each step are in the [runner design](../design/runner.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:12
msgid "Source: [`src/runner/runner.mbt`](../../../src/runner/runner.mbt), [`src/runner/bench_spec.mbt`](../../../src/runner/bench_spec.mbt)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:30
msgid "The examples on this page call them through their default aliases (`@model`, `@event`, `@runner`, `@fixture`, `@experiment`, `@async`); `fixture` and `experiment` are needed only to build a case with `BenchSpec::advanced`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:34
msgid "Asynchrony and type parameters"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:36
msgid "`run` and `execute_operation` are `async`. Call them from an `async fn main` or an `async test`; the `moonbitlang/async` runtime that drives them is available on the native, JS and wasm targets, not on wasm-gc. Subprocess workers need the native target. Like every `async` function, `run` may raise; the error it raises itself is `RunConfigError`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:42
msgid "The type parameters recur throughout the package:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:44
msgid "Parameter"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:46
msgid "the size parameter of a dataset, for example `Int`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:47
msgid "the value a fixture materializes for one dataset"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:48
msgid "the value an implementation runs on, produced by the fixture's `prepare`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:49
msgid "what the reference oracle computes"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:50
msgid "what an implementation returns"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:51
msgid "state threaded from one operation to the next (`Unit` when stateless)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:52
msgid "accumulator and final value of the `OutputSink`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:54
msgid "Running a plan"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:58
msgid "`run` executes a compiled plan and returns the run summary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:64
msgid "Before anything runs, `run` checks the plan against the protocol of the context and raises `RunConfigError` when they do not fit together (a `PerRun` fixture with more than one dataset, see `BenchSpec::compile`); nothing is emitted in that case."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:69
msgid "For every scale, in the order of the case, the runner goes through the datasets of that scale. The protocol's `experiment_design` decides how many there are and which blocks measure each. With $E$ = `exploratory_samples`, $C$ = `confirmatory_samples` and $r$ = `repeats_per_dataset`, the blocks of a scale are numbered $b = 0, \\dots, E + C - 1$ (exploratory first) and"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:75
msgid "Datasets per scale $D$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:75
msgid "Dataset index of block $b$"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:81
msgid "The dataset with index $j$ of the scale at position $s$ has `dataset_id` $= s \\cdot D + j$, unique within the run. For each dataset the runner materializes the input, validates the implementations against the oracle if `validation_coverage` asks for it, warms up and calibrates every implementation if it is the first dataset of the scale (the batch sizes then apply to all datasets of the scale), and measures the dataset's blocks: one batch per implementation per block, in the order of `balanced_order`. Every validation, failure, calibration and observation is sent to the sink as it happens; the summary is sent last, and the location string returned by the sink's `finish` becomes `artifact_location` of the returned summary."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:92
msgid "The returned `RunSummary` has `complete == true`, the event counts, the validation tallies (`passed_count`, `failed_count`, `unsupported_count`, `expected_difference_count`, `measurement_validation_count`), the environment, the protocol and the seed. Its `run_id` is `@model.run_identity(case_id, protocol, seed, environment.provenance)`, so it is unique when the environment's provenance `run_id` is (`@env_detect.detect` makes a fresh one)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:100
msgid "A validation failure does not stop the measurement: the implementation is still timed and the failure is reported, so that a report can show the mismatch next to the series it invalidates."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:141
msgid "With `QuickCheck` there is one exploratory and three confirmatory block per scale, so two scales and one implementation give eight observations. The example writes the environment by hand so that the run id is the same every time; a real run would use `@env_detect.detect`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:148
msgid "`RunContext` carries everything a run needs besides the plan."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:160
msgid "Note the argument order of `RunContext::new`: environment, sink, seed, protocol. The seed is passed unchanged to the fixture through `GenerationContext.seed`, is mixed into the block order (see `balanced_order`), and is recorded in `RunSummary.seed` and the run id."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:167
msgid "`balanced_order` returns the cyclic rotation of implementation indices used for one block."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:174
msgid "`balanced_order(k, b)` is $[(0 + b) \\bmod k, (1 + b) \\bmod k, \\dots, (k-1+b) \\bmod k]$. Under `OrderPolicy::BalancedBlocks(order_seed)` the runner uses block offset $b + o$ with $o = (\\text{order\\_seed} \\oplus \\text{run\\_seed}) \\bmod k$; under `FixedOrder` it uses the identity. Over any $k$ consecutive blocks every implementation occupies every position exactly once."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:188
msgid "Protocols"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:192
msgid "`ValidatedProtocol` is a `RunProtocol` that passed `validate_protocol`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:200
msgid "It can only be obtained from `validate_protocol` or a `ProtocolPreset`, so a plan never runs with a negative sample count or an empty iteration range."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:205
msgid "`validate_protocol` checks a protocol and returns either a `ValidatedProtocol` or every violation found."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:212
msgid "Rule"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:212
msgid "Error"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:215
msgid "`warmup_time_us`, when set, finite and `>= 0`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:218
msgid "`target_batch_time_us` finite and `> 0`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:219
msgid "`max_sample_time_us` finite and `> 0`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:222
msgid "`practical_delta_pct` finite and `>= 0`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:224
msgid "under `HierarchicalDatasetsAndRepeats`, $r$ divides `exploratory_samples`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:225
msgid "under `HierarchicalDatasetsAndRepeats`, $r$ divides `confirmatory_samples`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:226
msgid "under the other designs, `repeats_per_dataset == 1`"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:228
msgid "All rules are checked; the errors are returned together, in this order. The divisibility rules are checked only when `repeats_per_dataset >= 1`. They make every dataset of a hierarchical design belong to exactly one phase: with $r$ dividing $E$, blocks $0, \\dots, E-1$ fill whole datasets, so no dataset is measured partly by exploratory and partly by confirmatory blocks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:234
msgid "\"Finite\" excludes `NaN` and both infinities. Without that requirement a `NaN` would pass every rule, because every comparison with `NaN` is false: a `NaN` `target_batch_time_us` would disable calibration and a `NaN` `practical_delta_pct` would make every later comparison meaningless. A threshold of `0.0` is valid; `stats.compare_paired` then reports every non-zero relative delta as `Faster` or `Slower` and an exact tie as `Equivalent`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:284
msgid "`ProtocolConfigError` names one violated protocol rule."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:297
msgid "The `String` payload is the field name; the number is the rejected value. `IndivisibleSamples(field, samples, repeats)` carries the sample count and the `repeats_per_dataset` that does not divide it; `UnusedRepeatsPerDataset(r)` carries a `repeats_per_dataset` other than `1` under a design that does not use it."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:305
msgid "`ProtocolPreset` names a ready-made protocol."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:317
msgid "`validated` returns the preset's protocol (for `Custom`, the wrapped one). The presets are:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:326
msgid "batch iterations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:326
msgid "1 to 1000"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:326
msgid "1 to 10000"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:326
msgid "5 to 10000"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:336
msgid "datasets per scale"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:336
msgid "5 (1 exploratory, 4 confirmatory)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:338
msgid "The runner acts on every field except `outlier_policy` and `practical_delta_pct`, which are analysis settings: they are recorded in the summary, and `report.document_from_jsonl` applies them when it compares implementations. `RegressionGate` measures five datasets per scale, five consecutive blocks each, and validates every measured batch; its first dataset is measured only by the exploratory blocks."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:345
msgid "Implementations"
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:349
msgid "`Implementation` is one competitor in a benchmark."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:361
msgid "`id` names it in events and must be unique and non-empty within a case; `version` is copied into every observation and validation. `initial_context` starts each validation sequence and each measured batch. `synchronize` is called immediately before the clock starts and after it stops; use it to wait for queued asynchronous work (a GPU stream, a thread pool)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:369
msgid "`Implementation::stateless` wraps a function of the prepared input that needs no context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:376
msgid "The `next_context` of the function's result is ignored and replaced by `Some(())`, so a stateless operation never ends a sequence early."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:381
msgid "`Implementation::in_process` builds an implementation that runs in the current process and threads a context."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:388
msgid "Return `next_context = Some(c)` to continue with `c`; return `None` to end the sequence (in a timed batch this marks the observation invalid)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:403
msgid "`Implementation::worker` builds an implementation that runs each operation in a separate process."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:410
msgid "Use it for code that may crash, hang or corrupt memory. A crash becomes an `Aborted` outcome instead of ending the benchmark."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:415
msgid "`ExecutionMode` says where an operation runs."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:424
msgid "It is readonly outside the package; build it through the `Implementation` constructors."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:429
msgid "`WorkerSpec` describes how to run one operation as a subprocess."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:442
msgid "`build_arguments` produces the argument vector, `decode` turns the worker's stdout into a result, `timeout_ms` defaults to `5000`, and `cwd` defaults to the current directory."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:468
msgid "`execute_operation` runs one operation of an implementation."
msgstr ""

#: manual/api/runner.md:474
msgid "For `InProcess` it calls the function. For `Subprocess` it starts the command, captures stdout and stderr, and maps the result:"
