msgid "An in-place sort destroys its input. The fixture copies the generated array before every batch (`PerBatch`) and keeps that copy outside the timed region (`ExcludedFromMeasurement`):"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:189
msgid "Without `clone_input` the second batch would sort an already sorted array and measure a different workload. To include the copy in the measurement, use `IncludedInMeasurement`; to copy before every single operation, use `PerIteration`. The [runner design](../design/runner.md) lists exactly what each combination puts inside the clock."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:195
msgid "Validate a stateful sequence"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:197
msgid "Operations that carry state, such as a running total, are validated as a sequence. The implementation and the reference oracle each thread their own context, and `sequence_length` sets how many steps are compared:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:256
msgid "Step 4 of the sequence returns $3 \\cdot 5 = 15$, and the evidence records both the value and the context after the step."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:259
msgid "For a reference oracle, `sequence_length(input)` must return a positive value equal to the case length shown above. The runner uses that value for both sequences. A disagreement is a `RunConfigError`; an implementation or oracle that stops early after passing steps produces an explicit invalid length-mismatch result. This prevents the shared prefix from being mistaken for a complete check."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:266
msgid "Catch and minimize a wrong implementation"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:268
msgid "An off-by-one implementation fails validation. With a shrinker, the runner reduces the failing input before writing the failure artifact:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:306
msgid "The failure event carries the seed, the original and minimal fingerprints, the shrink path and the replay command `identity-worker off-by-one 40`. The implementation is still timed; the report hides its series and shows the mismatch instead."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:311
msgid "Measure several random datasets per scale"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:313
msgid "One fixed dataset per scale measures one input very precisely; a result may still depend on that particular input. With random inputs, measure several datasets per scale. `HierarchicalDatasetsAndRepeats` gives every `repeats_per_dataset` consecutive blocks a fresh dataset, so the run sees several inputs and still repeats each one. The generator must use the dataset: `GenerationContext.seed` is the run seed for every dataset, and `dataset_key.dataset_id` tells them apart."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:397
msgid "Each scale has $2 + 6 = 8$ blocks and $8 / 2 = 4$ datasets, so two scales and two implementations give 32 observations. Block $b$ of a scale measures the dataset with index $\\lfloor b/2 \\rfloor$; the ids continue across scales (scale `1000` has datasets 4 to 7). The first dataset of each scale is measured only by the two exploratory blocks, and it is where warmup and calibration run. `ConfirmatoryOnly` validates the three datasets that confirmatory blocks measure: 3 datasets, 2 scales, 2 implementations, one step each, 12 validations. `validate_protocol` insists that 2 divides both sample counts, so no dataset spans both phases."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:407
msgid "The other choices of the protocol work the same way:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:409
msgid "To"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:409
msgid "Set"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:411
msgid "measure one dataset per block"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:411
msgid "`MultipleDatasetsSingleMeasurement` (and `repeats_per_dataset` 1)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:412
msgid "validate every dataset, including exploratory ones"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:413
msgid "also validate right before every measured batch"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:413
msgid "`ValidationCoverage::EveryMeasurement` (validations carry the batch's `MeasurementKey`)"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:415
msgid "Validation never runs inside the timed region, whatever the coverage; more coverage costs wall-clock time, not accuracy."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:418
msgid "Write your own protocol"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:420
msgid "Presets cover common cases. For a gate in CI you may want more confirmatory blocks, a multiple of the number of implementations so that every rotation cycle is complete:"
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:445
msgid "With two implementations, $2 + 30 = 32$ blocks form 16 complete cycles. The identity is a digest of every field; changing any of them, even the order seed, gives another one."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:451
msgid "**Isolate unsafe code.** `Implementation::worker` runs each operation in a child process with a timeout; a crash becomes an `Aborted` outcome and a hang a `Timeout`, both counted as infrastructure failures. Workers need the native target. See [`WorkerSpec`](../api/runner.md#workerspec)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:456
msgid "**Asynchronous devices.** Pass `synchronize=` to `Implementation::in_process` with a function that blocks until queued work is done. The runner calls it right before starting and right before stopping the clock."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:460
msgid "**Equal work per batch.** `BatchPolicy::SharedBatchSize` gives every implementation the smallest calibrated batch size."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:463
msgid "**Several implementations against each other.** Use `OracleSpec::Relational` or `ReferenceAndRelational` to compare implementations pairwise when no single reference exists; see the [experiment tutorial](experiment.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:468
msgid "**Reproducible inputs.** Derive dataset seeds inside the fixture with `@generator.derive_seed(context.seed, context.case_id, context.dataset_key.dataset_id)`; see the [generator tutorial](generator.md)."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:474
msgid "**Work inside the payload that is not the payload.** Allocation, parsing or printing inside the implementation function is timed. Move it into the fixture."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:477
msgid "**Forgetting `clone_input` for mutating code.** Later batches then measure a different input."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:479
msgid "**Reading timings from invalid observations.** Filter on `valid` and drop datasets with validation failures before comparing."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:481
msgid "**Mixing phases.** Exploratory blocks are for orientation; decide on confirmatory ones."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:483
msgid "**Reusing a provenance run id.** `RunSummary.run_id` is built from the case, the protocol identity, the seed and the provenance run id and timestamp; a hand-written snapshot reused for every run gives every run of a case the same id. `@env_detect.detect` makes a fresh provenance run id each time."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:487
msgid "**The same input in every dataset.** Under `MultipleDatasetsSingleMeasurement` and `HierarchicalDatasetsAndRepeats` the fixture is called once per dataset with the same run seed; derive the input from `dataset_key.dataset_id`, otherwise every dataset is identical."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:491
msgid "**`PerRun` setup with several datasets.** A run-wide prepared value can come from only one dataset, so `compile` and `run` reject it; use `PerDataset`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:493
msgid "**Calling `run` outside an async context.** It is an `async fn`."
msgstr ""

#: manual/tutorial/runner.md:494
msgid "**A zero threshold with noisy data.** `validate_protocol` accepts `0.0`, and `stats.compare_paired` then calls every non-zero difference `Faster` or `Slower`. Choose the smallest change you care about."
msgstr ""

